>
<h4 id=3D"heading-writing-code-in-jupyter-notebook" class=3D"g-md-h4">Writi=
ng Code in Jupyter Notebook</h4>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">In this section, we will go through some examples of ho=
w to write code in Jupyter Notebook. This section is a preview of how noteb=
ooks work. For more on Jupyter=E2=80=99s useful features, check out <a targ=
et=3D"_blank" href=3D"https://jupyter-notebook.readthedocs.io/en/stable/not=
ebook.html" class=3D"g-md-a gamut-1r92bn9-AnchorBase e14vpv2g0" rel=3D"noop=
ener">the documentation</a>.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">When you write code in Jupyter Notebook, you will need =
to run each cell individually. Start with a basic print statement: <code cl=
ass=3D"g-md-code">print("hello world")</code>. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Notice that the result is printed below the cell. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"notebook showing print(&quot;hello world&qu=
ot;)" class=3D"g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/=
data-science-career-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-hello-world.=
png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Let=E2=80=99s try another one. This time, we will assig=
n variables <code class=3D"g-md-code">x =3D 2</code> and <code class=3D"g-m=
d-code">y =3D 3</code>. When we run this cell, nothing appears in the outpu=
t. Why?</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"notebook of variables x =3D 2, y =3D 3" cla=
ss=3D"g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/data-scie=
nce-career-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-enter-variables.png">=
</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Assigning variables doesn=E2=80=99t generate any output=
, so there is no result to write. We know the cell has finished running bec=
ause a number appeared in the <code class=3D"g-md-code">[   ]</code> (this =
is a temporary cell number). Let=E2=80=99s try using our <code class=3D"g-m=
d-code">x</code> and <code class=3D"g-md-code">y</code> variables. We can a=
dd them together. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Enter <code class=3D"g-md-code">x + y</code> in the nex=
t cell. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"notebook with In[2]: x=3D2\ny=3D3, In[3]: x=
 + y, Out[3]: 5" class=3D"g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT=
.com/Paths/data-science-career-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-o=
utput-run.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Great! We got our expected value: 5. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Notice that we didn=E2=80=99t use a <code class=3D"g-md=
-code">print()</code> statement, but Jupyter Notebook wrote the result anyh=
ow.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Jupyter Notebook will only write the result of the last=
 line but will print anything contained in a print statement no matter wher=
e it occurs. This can be confusing at first, so let=E2=80=99s look at an ex=
ample. In the next cell, enter:</p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">x</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> +&nbsp;</span><span class=3D"m=
tk9">y</span></span><br><span><span class=3D"mtk9">x</span><span class=3D"m=
tk1"> -&nbsp;</span><span class=3D"mtk9">y</span></span><br><span><span></s=
pan></span><br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"notebook showing output of x + y not being =
displayed" class=3D"g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/P=
aths/data-science-career-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-output-=
not-run.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">This time only <code class=3D"g-md-code">-1</code> was =
returned. This is because Jupyter Notebook only displays the last line. If =
you want to display both, you have to wrap the first one in a <code class=
=3D"g-md-code">print()</code> statement.</p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk1">print(</span><span class=3D"mtk9">x</span><span class=3D"mtk=
1"> +&nbsp;</span><span class=3D"mtk9">y</span><span class=3D"mtk1">)</span=
></span><br><span><span class=3D"mtk9">x</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> -&nbsp=
;</span><span class=3D"mtk9">y</span></span><br><span><span></span></span><=
br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"notebook showing x + y =3D 5 printing and o=
utput" class=3D"g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths=
/data-science-career-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-output-and-=
run.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Try printing both lines on your own.</p>
<h4 id=3D"heading-jupyter-notebook-basic-features" class=3D"g-md-h4">Jupyte=
r Notebook basic features</h4>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Let=E2=80=99s work with some more complicated code. The=
 purpose of this section is to try out some basic Jupyter Notebook features=
. If we use a function that you have not learned about yet, don=E2=80=99t w=
orry, this section is only an example. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Let=E2=80=99s work with some data. We have a list of nu=
mbers: <code class=3D"g-md-code">mynums =3D [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</code></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Input this list into a cell and return the output. </p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">mynums</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> =3D&nbsp;[</span><span cl=
ass=3D"mtk9">1</span><span class=3D"mtk1">, </span><span class=3D"mtk9">2</=
span><span class=3D"mtk1">, </span><span class=3D"mtk9">3</span><span class=
=3D"mtk1">, </span><span class=3D"mtk9">4</span><span class=3D"mtk1">, </sp=
an><span class=3D"mtk9">5</span><span class=3D"mtk1">]</span></span><br><sp=
an><span class=3D"mtk9">mynums</span></span><br><span><span></span></span><=
br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"mynums listed in a notebook cell" class=3D"=
g-md-img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/data-science-ca=
reer-path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-sample-data.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Now let=E2=80=99s try printing each item to the console=
. </p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk12">for</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mtk9"=
>n</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mtk12">in</span><span =
class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mtk9">mynums</span><span class=3D"mtk=
1">:</span></span><br><span><span class=3D"mtk1">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;pr=
int(</span><span class=3D"mtk9">n</span><span class=3D"mtk1">+</span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">1</span><span class=3D"mtk1">)</span></span><br><span><span>=
</span></span><br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"for n in mynums: print(n+1) loop displayed =
in a notebook cell with the output displayed below the cell" class=3D"g-md-=
img" src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/data-science-career-=
path/getting-started-off-platform/notebook-sample-loop.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Congratulations!! You are ready to start working with d=
ata in Jupyter Notebook. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">The remainder of this article will introduce debugging =
and accessing documentation. You don=E2=80=99t need to know about these to =
start using Jupyter Notebook on your own. However, these features will supp=
ort you on your learning journey (and beyond).</p>
<h4 id=3D"heading-help-ful-features" class=3D"g-md-h4">Help-ful Features</h=
4>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Jupyter Notebook has lots of built-in tools to help you=
 learn to code. We will explore three of them here (the help documentation =
with <code class=3D"g-md-code">?</code>, tab completion, and how to access =
the function arguments). This is just the beginning, check out this section=
 of the <a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"https://jakevdp.github.io/PythonDataSc=
ienceHandbook/01.01-help-and-documentation.html" class=3D"g-md-a gamut-1r92=
bn9-AnchorBase e14vpv2g0" rel=3D"noopener">Python Data Science Handbook</a>=
 for more tools.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Jupyter Notebook has a generalized help function with t=
he <code class=3D"g-md-code">?</code> character. Let=E2=80=99s say you want=
 to know what object type <code class=3D"g-md-code">mynums</code> is. You c=
an use the <code class=3D"g-md-code">?</code> to get full documentation abo=
ut it. </p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">mynums?</span></span><br><span><span></span></span><br></div=
></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"help '?' character used on mynums in a note=
book cell, shows that mynums is a list with 5 objects" class=3D"g-md-img" s=
rc=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/data-science-career-path/g=
etting-started-off-platform/notebook-help-list.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Here you can see the object type, a preview of the data=
, and general information. You can do the same for functions such as <code =
class=3D"g-md-code">len</code> or <code class=3D"g-md-code">sum</code>. Go =
ahead and try it in your own Notebook.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Jupyter Notebook also offers tab completion. Tab comple=
tion means that you can start typing, hit the <kbd class=3D"g-md-kbd">Tab</=
kbd> key, and Jupyter Notebook will offer suggestions about what could come=
 next. For example, to turn our list into a pandas DataFrame, we can use th=
e <code class=3D"g-md-code">.DataFrame()</code> function. But you might for=
get how it is spelled (i.e., is it =E2=80=98f=E2=80=99 or =E2=80=98F=E2=80=
=99?). The tab function to the rescue! Let=E2=80=99s try it out. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">First, import pandas.</p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk12">import</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mt=
k9">pandas</span><span class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mtk12">as</spa=
n><span class=3D"mtk1"> </span><span class=3D"mtk9">pd</span></span><br><sp=
an><span></span></span><br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Then type <code class=3D"g-md-code">D</code> and hit th=
e <kbd class=3D"g-md-kbd">Tab</kbd> button to see your options.</p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">pd</span><span class=3D"mtk1">.</span><span class=3D"mtk10">=
D</span></span><br><span><span></span></span><br></div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img alt=3D"tab completion showing pd.D and DataFrame, =
DataOffset, DatetimeIndex, and DatetimeTZDtype options" class=3D"g-md-img" =
src=3D"https://static-assets.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/Paths/data-science-career-path/=
getting-started-off-platform/notebook-tab-completion.png"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">You can keep typing or select <code class=3D"g-md-code"=
>DataFrame</code> from the list. Now let=E2=80=99s finish our function:</p>
<pre class=3D"g-md-pre"><pre class=3D"gamut-mu6hz5 e1rzuoz70"><code><div da=
ta-lang=3D"AI Academy by RiWoT-python" class=3D"gamut-1rlug0v epnovk10"><span><span =
class=3D"mtk9">pd</span><span class=3D"mtk1">.</span><span class=3D"mtk10">=
DataFrame</span><span class=3D"mtk1">(</span><span class=3D"mtk9">mynums</s=
pan><span class=3D"mtk1">)</span></span><br><span><span></span></span><br><=
/div></code></pre></pre>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">That=E2=80=99s great, but sometimes you might know the =
function=E2=80=99s name but forget what arguments it needs.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Fortunately, Jupyter Notebook has a solution for that t=
oo. For example, you want to sort your list from largest to smallest. You k=
now you need the <code class=3D"g-md-code">.sort()</code> function, but you=
 forgot what arguments you need to call. Place your cursor between the <cod=
